+//! The vector and its allocation header have the same layout as that crate's
+//! vector, so every allocation has the same size and alignment. Because the
+//! count is not atomic, a vector and all of its clones must stay on the
+//! thread that created them; the type is neither `Send` nor `Sync`.
+//!
+//! A vector whose `IMMORTAL_ALLOWED` parameter is true may instead hold an
+//! immortal header: one that is never freed and whose count is never
+//! written, so clones on any thread share it. Only string storage sets the
+//! parameter.
